<Base title="Name Register"
      eyebrow="Every Lerena encountered anywhere, placed or not"
      dek="A working register of every person of this surname met in the course of the research — with where they were found, and an honest mark saying whether they connect to our line, do not, or simply cannot yet be told either way.">

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Why keep this</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The surname is rare. Forebears counts about <strong>2,124 Lerenas worldwide</strong>, with the
      highest density anywhere in <strong>Uruguay</strong> — the signature of a small founding group that
      emigrated. At that scale, most people carrying the name probably <em>are</em> related to one another
      at some depth. But &ldquo;probably related&rdquo; is not evidence, and this archive does not publish
      guesses as findings.</p>
    <p>So every Lerena met along the way is written down here rather than discarded, with the record that
      produced them. None is claimed as kin. The moment Pablo Armando&rsquo;s parents are named, this list
      becomes the place to look — several of these people may snap into position at once, and the work of
      finding them again will already have been done.</p>
    <p class="cap">Three marks are used. <strong>Connected</strong> — proven to the direct line.
      <strong>Excluded</strong> — checked and shown not to belong. <strong>Unplaced</strong> — found, recorded,
      and honestly unresolved. Most are unplaced, and that is the point of the page.</p>
  </div>
</section>

