<section class="blk">
  <h2>The ones that matter most</h2>
  <p class="dek">Unplaced, but for reasons worth stating: the right country, the right generation, or the
    right trade.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px"><table><thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Born</th><th>Nationality</th><th>Where found</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Gilberto LERENA (Gilberto Justiniano LERENA LENGUAS)</strong><br><span class="cap">Co-founder of the Argentine Stud Book; a Group 1 at Palermo renamed for him in 1914. A Lerena at the centre of Argentine thoroughbred breeding, in the right country at the right time. No proven link to Pablo Armando.</span></td><td class="m">1854</td><td>Uruguayan/Argentine</td><td>1895 Argentine national census, Seccion 21</td><td><span class="chip lore">Unplaced — high interest</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Carlos LERENA</strong><br><span class="cap">Travelling with Rosa M. E. Lerena, an Argentine. Born within three years of Pablo Armando, Uruguayan.</span></td><td class="m">1885</td><td>URUGUAYAN</td><td>CEMLA, ship WESTERN WORLD</td><td><span class="chip inf">Unplaced — of interest</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Rosa M. E. LERENA</strong><br><span class="cap">Travelling with Carlos; evidently his wife.</span></td><td class="m">1887</td><td>Argentine</td><td>CEMLA, ship WESTERN WORLD</td><td><span class="chip inf">Unplaced — of interest</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Gilberto LERENA</strong><br><span class="cap">An ARGENTINE-national Lerena born within three years of Pablo Armando, carrying the Gilberto name of the Stud Book family. Travelling with a son of the same name. Not in the 1895 census household of Gilberto (1854).</span></td><td class="m">1879</td><td>ARGENTINE</td><td>CEMLA, ship CAP. NORTE</td><td><span class="chip lore">Unplaced — high interest</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Josefina LLERENA</strong><br><span class="cap">An Argentine woman of property who crossed at least three times, single throughout. Born 1868.</span></td><td class="m">1868</td><td>ARGENTINE</td><td>CEMLA, three crossings</td><td><span class="chip inf">Unplaced — of interest</span></td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px"><strong>The Gilberto thread is the one to pull.</strong>
    Gilberto Justiniano Lerena Lenguas — Uruguayan-born, heading a household in Buenos Aires at the 1895
    census — was a <strong>co-founder of the Argentine Stud Book</strong>, and a Group 1 race at Palermo was
    renamed for him in 1914. The family story has Pablo Armando bringing Argentine horse breeding to South
    Africa. A Lerena at the centre of Argentine thoroughbred breeding, in the right country in the right
    decade, is either the explanation or a coincidence of a very particular kind. A <em>second</em> Gilberto
    Lerena — Argentine, born about 1879, in commerce — arrives at Buenos Aires in 1928 with a son of the
    same name, and is not among the children listed in the 1895 household. Neither is yet linked to us.</div>
</section>

